diff --git a/bins/ios/README.md b/bins/ios/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..af20f83
--- /dev/null
+++ b/bins/ios/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,50 @@
+# bins/ios — Concord as an iOS static library
+
+`libconcord_ios.a` for a `NEPacketTunnelProvider` (NetworkExtension) target,
+mirroring `bins/android` (`libconcord.so`). Upstream commit:
+`5f1d0903775f5606bfa938cc0bbfedc25031ed6a` (rebased 2026-11-17 from 187ac9d)..
+
+## Layout
+
+| File | What it is |
+|---|---|
+| `Cargo.toml` | `crate-type = ["staticlib", "rlib"]`, lib name `concord_ios`, same core deps as `bins/android` + `libc` (for the socketpair bridge). |
+| `src/lib.rs` | Crate docs + re-exports. |
+| `src/abi.rs` | `concord_start() -> i32` — builds the core once per process (no thread hook; there is no JVM on iOS). |
+| `src/core.rs` | `Core`: multi-thread tokio runtime, `EngineConfig { android: false, … }`, capabilities `["tun.inherited", "tun.channel"]` (NOT `tun.device`), `ControlOptions { privileged: false, profile_controller: false }`. |
+| `src/platform.rs` | `IosPlatform` implementing `concord_platform::Platform` directly. No `PlatformHost` trait, no `concord_set_platform_host`: `protect` is a no-op returning `true`, `find_owner` returns `None` (iOS exposes no per-flow owner to an extension), `default_interface`/`system_dns` are empty, `fd_pressure` uses `FdPressure` as on Android. |
+| `src/log.rs` | `eprintln!` logging (same fallback as Android's logcat off-Android). |
+| `src/bridge.rs` | Option A socketpair bridge: `concord_ios_bridge_open/close`. See its docs for the four caveats and the verdict (sound in principle, second choice behind Option B). |
+| `concord_ios.h` | Hand-written C header for the whole C ABI (see its top comment on why hand-written, and that cbindgen would be better long-term). |
+
+## JSON-RPC flow
+
+`core.hello` → `core.init {"home": "<app-group dir>"}` (`sdk_int` unset → 0)
+→ `config.apply` → `engine.start` → `tun.start`. `core.hello` reports
+`"os": "ios"` automatically (`EngineConfig.android == false` →
+`std::env::consts::OS`, `crates/api/src/control.rs:589-594`).
+
+TUN sources on iOS: `{"kind": "channel", "handle": H}` (Option B, preferred)
+or `{"kind": "inherited", "handle": H}` (Option A socketpair). Never
+`{"kind": "device"}` — the core must not create its own TUN device inside a
+NetworkExtension.
+
+## Depends on the Option B patch
+
+This crate is written against the patched tree:
+
+* `crates/tun/src/channel.rs` + `DeviceSource::Channel` + `TunSource::channel`
+  + `concord_packet_channel_*` FFI (the Option B patch) — for `"tun.channel"`.
+* The `TunDevice::wrap` iOS compile fix in `crates/tun/src/device/mod.rs`
+  (the generic unix `DeviceImpl` has no `name()`/`if_index()`, so the
+  unpatched `wrap()` does not compile for `aarch64-apple-ios` at all).
+* `Cargo.toml` workspace members must include `"bins/ios"`.
+
+## First gate (unverified)
+
+`cargo build --target aarch64-apple-ios -p concord-ios` on a macOS runner
+with the iphoneos SDK. BoringSSL 5.4 (`boring-sys`, cmake + bindgen) and
+`aws-lc-sys` for `aarch64-apple-ios` have never been built in this workspace;
+if they fail, nothing else matters. Then prove the packet bridge on macOS
+before wiring the NE extension (see `TUN_INVESTIGATION.md` §7 in the
+scaffold repo).
diff --git a/bins/ios/concord_ios.h b/bins/ios/concord_ios.h
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..c16ea87
--- /dev/null
+++ b/bins/ios/concord_ios.h
@@ -0,0 +1,116 @@
+/* concord_ios.h — C API of libconcord_ios.a (bins/ios).
+ *
+ * Hand-written: there is no cbindgen step in upstream Concord (no cbindgen
+ * config, no generated header for the Android .so either). A hand-written
+ * header is the deliverable; if cbindgen is ever added to the workspace it
+ * should generate this file from bins/ios + crates/ffi instead — cbindgen
+ * would be better long-term (it cannot drift from the Rust signatures), but
+ * it is a new workspace dependency and build step, so the hand-written
+ * header ships first and must be kept in sync by hand.
+ *
+ * The symbols fall into three groups:
+ *   1. concord_start — defined in bins/ios (src/abi.rs).
+ *   2. concord_open / concord_open_callback / concord_send / concord_close /
+ *      concord_adopt_fd — defined in crates/ffi (src/abi.rs), linked into
+ *      the staticlib through the bins/ios dependency on concord-ffi.
+ *   3. concord_packet_channel_* — defined in crates/ffi by the Option B
+ *      patch (packet channel). They only exist in a patched tree; linking
+ *      against an unpatched libconcord_ios.a leaves them unresolved.
+ *   4. concord_ios_bridge_* — Option A socketpair bridge, bins/ios
+ *      (src/bridge.rs). Works in a patched tree only as far as the
+ *      TunDevice::wrap iOS compile fix is applied (see bridge.rs).
+ *
+ * JSON-RPC flow over a session opened with concord_open_callback:
+ *   core.hello → core.init {"home": "<app-group dir>"} → config.apply →
+ *   engine.start → tun.start {"source": {"kind": "channel", "handle": H}}
+ *   (Option B) or {"source": {"kind": "inherited", "handle": H}} (Option A,
+ *   H from concord_ios_bridge_open). Sessions carry one JSON-RPC 2.0 message
+ *   per concord_send; replies/subscription events arrive via the Deliver
+ *   callback. See schema/api.json in the upstream tree for the full method
+ *   list. `core.init` takes no sdk_int on iOS (pass none → 0).
+ *
+ * Threading: the Deliver callback runs on a core thread, one message at a
+ * time; the bytes are valid only during the call. Copy them (e.g. into
+ * NSData) before returning. Return false from Deliver once the host is gone;
+ * that ends the session.
+ */
+
+#ifndef CONCORD_IOS_H
+#define CONCORD_IOS_H
+
+#include <stdbool.h>
+#include <stddef.h>
+#include <stdint.h>
+
+#ifdef __cplusplus
+extern "C" {
+#endif
+
+/* Starts the core, once per process. Returns 0 when it runs, -1 on failure.
+ * Later calls only report whether it runs. No thread hook (unlike Android:
+ * there is no JVM to attach). */
+int32_t concord_start(void);
+
+/* --- Sessions (crates/ffi) --- */
+
+/* Deliver callback: `data`/`len` are one complete JSON-RPC message, valid
+ * only during the call. Return false once the host is gone. */
+typedef bool (*ConcordDeliver)(void *context, const uint8_t *data, size_t len);
+/* Runs once when the core no longer holds the session (or right away when
+ * the open fails because the core is not up). May be NULL. */
+typedef void (*ConcordRelease)(void *context);
+
+/* Opens a session whose messages are handed to `deliver` with `context`.
+ * Returns the session id (never 0), or 0 when the core is not up. */
+uint64_t concord_open_callback(ConcordDeliver deliver, void *context,
+                               ConcordRelease release);
+
+/* Copies one complete JSON-RPC message onto a session. Returns 1 when the
+ * session exists, 0 when it does not. */
+int32_t concord_send(uint64_t session, const uint8_t *data, size_t len);
+
+/* Ends a session. Closing twice is harmless. */
+void concord_close(uint64_t session);
+
+/* Hands a descriptor to the core, which closes it. Returns the handle
+ * `tun.start` names as {"kind": "inherited", "handle": …}, or 0 when the
+ * core is not up (the descriptor is closed either way). */
+uint64_t concord_adopt_fd(int32_t fd);
+
+/* --- Option B packet channel (patched tree only) --- */
+
+/* Creates a packet channel and returns its handle (never 0), or 0 on
+ * failure. The handle is what `tun.start` names as
+ * {"kind": "channel", "handle": …}. */
+uint64_t concord_packet_channel_open(void);
+
+/* Writes one IP packet (bare, no utun header) into the channel for the
+ * stack to read. Returns 1 when queued, 0 when the ring is full (retry),
+ * -1 when the handle is unknown. */
+int32_t concord_packet_write(uint64_t handle, const uint8_t *data, size_t len);
+
+/* Reads one packet the stack wrote, non-blocking, into `buf` (capacity
+ * `cap`). Returns the byte count, 0 when no packet is waiting, -1 when the
+ * handle is unknown or `buf` is NULL. Poll from the packetFlow pump loop. */
+int32_t concord_packet_read(uint64_t handle, uint8_t *buf, size_t cap);
+
+/* Drops a channel: the device end is released (a running tun.start keeps
+ * its own reference) and the packet end stops accepting packets. */
+void concord_packet_channel_close(uint64_t handle);
+
+/* --- Option A socketpair bridge (bins/ios) --- */
+
+/* Creates a SOCK_DGRAM socketpair, adopts one end into the core, returns 0
+ * and writes the host-side fd to `host_fd` and the tun.start handle to
+ * `handle`; returns -1 on failure. Pump bare IP packets through `host_fd`
+ * with send/recv (the core adds/strips the 4-byte utun header itself). */
+int32_t concord_ios_bridge_open(int32_t *host_fd, uint64_t *handle);
+
+/* Closes the host end of a bridge. The core end is owned by the core. */
+void concord_ios_bridge_close(int32_t host_fd);
+
+#ifdef __cplusplus
+}
+#endif
+
+#endif /* CONCORD_IOS_H */
diff --git a/bins/ios/src/abi.rs b/bins/ios/src/abi.rs
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..2c10f06
--- /dev/null
+++ b/bins/ios/src/abi.rs
@@ -0,0 +1,46 @@
+use std::panic::{AssertUnwindSafe, catch_unwind};
+use std::sync::OnceLock;
+
+use concord_api::LogBridge;
+use tracing_subscriber::layer::SubscriberExt;
+
+use crate::core::Core;
+use crate::log;
+
+/// Everything one `libconcord_ios.a` holds: the runtime that carries the flows,
+/// the control surface on it, and the hub through which the extension opens sessions.
+static CORE: OnceLock<Option<Core>> = OnceLock::new();
+
+/// Starts the core, once per process; later calls only report whether it runs.
+/// Returns 0 when the core runs, -1 when it could not start.
+///
+/// Unlike Android's `concord_start` there is no thread hook: there is no JVM
+/// to attach, and the core's threads need no per-thread host setup.
+#[unsafe(no_mangle)]
+pub extern "C" fn concord_start() -> i32 {
+    let started = catch_unwind(AssertUnwindSafe(|| CORE.get_or_init(start).is_some()));
+    if started.unwrap_or(false) { 0 } else { -1 }
+}
+
+fn start() -> Option<Core> {
+    std::panic::set_hook(Box::new(|info| log::error(&format!("panic: {info}"))));
+    #[cfg(unix)]
+    concord_platform::ignore_sigpipe();
+    concord_platform::raise_fd_limit();
+    match Core::new() {
+        Ok(core) => {
+            let bus = core.control().bus();
+            let bridge = LogBridge::new(bus.gate().clone(), bus.clone(), false);
+            let _ = tracing::subscriber::set_global_default(
+                tracing_subscriber::registry().with(bridge),
+            );
+            concord_ffi::set_notice(log::info);
+            concord_ffi::install(core.hub().clone());
+            Some(core)
+        }
+        Err(err) => {
+            log::error(&format!("runtime: {err}"));
+            None
+        }
+    }
+}
diff --git a/bins/ios/src/bridge.rs b/bins/ios/src/bridge.rs
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..594ba1e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/bins/ios/src/bridge.rs
@@ -0,0 +1,144 @@
+//! Option A bridge (experiment, no upstream change beyond the
+//! `TunDevice::wrap` iOS compile fix): a datagram socketpair whose one end is
+//! adopted by the core, so `packetFlow` packets can be pumped through it.
+//!
+//! Verdict: SOUND IN PRINCIPLE, BLOCKED AS A NO-UPSTREAM-CHANGE OPTION, and
+//! second choice behind Option B (the production packet channel). The four
+//! caveats, each with its exact code path:
+//!
+//! (i) Framing — SOCK_DGRAM, not SOCK_STREAM. The stack reads one packet per
+//! `poll_recv` (`crates/tun/src/device/mod.rs`, `Device::poll_recv`; the
+//! default `poll_recv_batch` calls `recv_one`, one packet per slot). A stream
+//! socketpair could return a partial or coalesced packet and corrupt the IP
+//! stream; a datagram socketpair preserves datagram boundaries, so one
+//! `recv` is one IP packet. `SOCK_DGRAM` is therefore required, and this
+//! helper creates the pair that way.
+//!
+//! (ii) The 4-byte utun header — the CORE adds/strips it, the host must NOT.
+//! On Apple targets the vendored tun-rs `Tun` wraps every read/write with a
+//! 4-byte packet-information header (`vendor/tun-rs/src/platform/unix/tun.rs`:
+//! `generate_packet_information()` returns `AF_INET`/`AF_INET6` as big-endian
+//! u32; `Tun::new` sets `ignore_packet_information = true` by default, and
+//! with it set `send()` prepends the header with `writev([header, packet])`
+//! and `recv()` strips it with `readv([head, buf])`). The iOS device path
+//! is the generic unix one (`crates/tun/src/device/mod.rs`: `other.rs`, not
+//! `apple.rs` which is `#[cfg(target_os = "macos")]` only), and that path
+//! goes through `Tun::send`/`recv`, so the header handling applies. The
+//! consequence for the host: the socketpair carries `header + IP packet` on
+//! the wire in both directions, and tun-rs does the translation — the Swift
+//! side writes BARE IP packets into its socketpair end and reads BARE IP
+//! packets out of it. (The name `ignore_packet_information` is misleading:
+//! `true` means "the caller ignores the header, tun-rs handles it". Nobody
+//! in Concord ever calls `set_ignore_packet_info`, so the default `true`
+//! holds. `DeviceBuilder::packet_information(false)` in `builder.rs:85`
+//! only affects devices built by the builder, not adopted fds.)
+//!
+//! (iii) tokio registration — a socket fd registers fine. `TunDevice::adopt`
+//! (`crates/tun/src/device/mod.rs:319-330`) calls
+//! `tun_rs::AsyncDevice::from_fd`, which goes through
+//! `vendor/tun-rs/src/async_device/unix/mod.rs:84-87` →
+//! `vendor/tun-rs/src/async_device/unix/mod.rs:84-87` →
+//! just wraps the fd in `Tun::new`) → `AsyncDevice::new_dev`
+//! (`vendor/tun-rs/src/async_device/unix/tokio.rs:255-259`), which registers
+//! the fd with `tokio::io::unix::AsyncFd::register`. `AsyncFd` only needs a
+//! pollable fd; a Unix datagram socket is pollable (readable when a datagram
+//! is queued). Nothing in that path checks that the fd is a TUN device — the
+//! `from_fd` docs say "must refer to a TUN/TAP device", but that is a
+//! framing contract (one datagram = one packet, which (i) and (ii) satisfy),
+//! not a kernel object check. UNVERIFIED on iOS: never exercised with a
+//! non-TUN fd; test on macOS first (pump a synthetic packet through).
+//!
+//! (iv) fd ownership / name lookup — tolerated, AFTER the wrap() patch.
+//! `TunDevice::adopt` takes ownership (`OwnedFd::into_raw_fd`, tun-rs closes
+//! it on failure) and `wrap()` reads `inner.name().unwrap_or_default()` and
+//! `inner.if_index().ok()`. On a socket fd a name lookup would fail, but the
+//! code tolerates failure (empty name, `None` index), and routes are a no-op
+//! on iOS anyway (`crates/tun/src/route/mod.rs`, `Os::Other` → `NullBackend`).
+//! BUT: on iOS the generic unix `DeviceImpl`
+//! (`vendor/tun-rs/src/platform/mod.rs`, "A TUN device for Android/iOS/…")
+//! defines NEITHER `name()` NOR `if_index()` — those exist only on the
+//! linux/macos/freebsd/openbsd/netbsd/windows impls — so `wrap()` does not
+//! compile for `aarch64-apple-ios` as-is. The Option B patch set therefore
+//! includes a minimal fix in `crates/tun/src/device/mod.rs` that cfg-gates
+//! the lookup to the targets that have it. Option A needs that one patch too;
+//! it is not a zero-patch option on iOS.
+//!
+//! Usage (Swift side, after `concord_start()` and `core.init`):
+//! 1. `concord_ios_bridge_open(&host_fd, &handle)` → host keeps `host_fd`.
+//! 2. `tun.start` with `{"source": {"kind": "inherited", "handle": handle}}`.
+//! 3. Pump: `packetFlow.readPackets()` → `send(host_fd, packet)`; `recv(host_fd)`
+//!    → `packetFlow.writePackets()`.
+//! 4. `tun.stop`, then `concord_ios_bridge_close(host_fd)`. The adopted end is
+//!    owned by the core and closed by it (including on every failure path).
+
+use std::ffi::{c_int, c_void};
+use std::os::fd::{FromRawFd, OwnedFd};
+
+/// Creates a `SOCK_DGRAM` Unix socketpair, adopts one end into the core, and
+/// hands the other end back. On success returns 0 and writes the host-side fd
+/// to `host_fd` and the `tun.start` handle to `handle`; on failure returns -1
+/// and closes both ends. `host_fd` / `handle` must be non-null.
+///
+/// # Safety
+/// Both pointers must be valid for writing.
+#[unsafe(no_mangle)]
+pub unsafe extern "C" fn concord_ios_bridge_open(host_fd: *mut c_int, handle: *mut u64) -> c_int {
+    if host_fd.is_null() || handle.is_null() {
+        return -1;
+    }
+    // SAFETY: the pointers were checked non-null by the caller contract.
+    unsafe {
+        *host_fd = -1;
+        *handle = 0;
+    }
+    let mut pair = [-1 as c_int, -1 as c_int];
+    // SOCK_DGRAM preserves datagram boundaries: one send = one IP packet on
+    // the core side (see the module docs, caveat (i)). SOCK_CLOEXEC so the
+    // fds do not leak into any spawned process.
+    let made = unsafe {
+        libc::socketpair(
+            libc::AF_UNIX,
+            libc::SOCK_DGRAM | libc::SOCK_CLOEXEC,
+            0,
+            pair.as_mut_ptr(),
+        )
+    };
+    if made != 0 {
+        return -1;
+    }
+    let [core_fd, host] = pair;
+    // Hand the core end to the core; `Hub::adopt_fd` takes ownership and
+    // returns 0 (closing the fd) when the core is not up.
+    let adopted = {
+        let fd = unsafe { OwnedFd::from_raw_fd(core_fd) };
+        match concord_ffi::hub() {
+            Some(hub) => hub.adopt_fd(fd),
+            None => 0,
+        }
+    };
+    if adopted == 0 {
+        // Core not up: `adopt_fd` already closed core_fd; close our end too.
+        unsafe { libc::close(host) };
+        return -1;
+    }
+    // SAFETY: the pointers were checked non-null above.
+    unsafe {
+        *host_fd = host;
+        *handle = adopted;
+    }
+    0
+}
+
+/// Closes the host end of a bridge opened by `concord_ios_bridge_open`.
+/// The core end is owned by the core. No-op for a negative fd.
+#[unsafe(no_mangle)]
+pub extern "C" fn concord_ios_bridge_close(host_fd: c_int) {
+    if host_fd >= 0 {
+        // SAFETY: `host_fd` is a fd this module handed out; closing it once.
+        unsafe { libc::close(host_fd) };
+    }
+}
+
+/// Unused marker so the `c_void` import in the header docs stays honest.
+#[allow(dead_code)]
+fn _unused(_: *mut c_void) {}
diff --git a/bins/ios/src/core.rs b/bins/ios/src/core.rs
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..288ad52
--- /dev/null
+++ b/bins/ios/src/core.rs
@@ -0,0 +1,78 @@
+use std::sync::Arc;
+
+use concord_api::{Control, ControlOptions, EventBus};
+use concord_engine::{Engine, EngineConfig};
+use concord_ffi::Hub;
+use concord_rpc::Server;
+use tokio::runtime::{Builder, Runtime};
+
+use crate::platform::IosPlatform;
+
+/// Capabilities the iOS host lets the core use. `tun.inherited` is the
+/// socketpair bridge (`concord_adopt_fd` + `tun.start` inherited);
+/// `tun.channel` is the Option B packet channel (`concord_packet_channel_open`
+/// + `tun.start` channel). `tun.device` is deliberately NOT claimed: the core
+/// must not create its own TUN device inside a NetworkExtension. There is no
+/// `host.protect` / `host.resolveUid`: iOS exposes no `VpnService`-style host
+/// callbacks (see `platform.rs`).
+const CAPABILITIES: [&str; 2] = ["tun.inherited", "tun.channel"];
+
+/// Everything one `libconcord_ios.a` holds: the runtime that carries the flows,
+/// the control surface on it, and the hub through which the extension opens sessions.
+pub struct Core {
+    _runtime: Runtime,
+    control: Arc<Control>,
+    hub: Arc<Hub>,
+}
+
+impl Core {
+    pub fn new() -> std::io::Result<Self> {
+        let workers = concord_platform::available_cpus().map_or(1, |count| count.get());
+        let mut builder = Builder::new_multi_thread();
+        builder
+            .worker_threads(workers)
+            .thread_name("concord")
+            .enable_all();
+        let runtime = builder.build()?;
+        let entered = runtime.enter();
+
+        let bus = EventBus::new();
+        let platform = Arc::new(IosPlatform::new());
+        let engine = Engine::new(EngineConfig {
+            sink: bus.clone(),
+            // iOS is not Android: `core.hello` reports "ios", `tun.device` is
+            // rejected only by the host never asking for it, `core.init`'s
+            // `sdk_int` stays 0, and `tun.stop` takes the non-Android path
+            // (`disable_tun` → `reconcile_tun` closes the runner slot).
+            android: false,
+            platform: platform.clone(),
+        });
+        let control = Control::new(
+            engine,
+            bus,
+            ControlOptions {
+                privileged: false,
+                capabilities: CAPABILITIES.map(String::from).to_vec(),
+                build: env!("CARGO_PKG_VERSION").to_owned(),
+                profile_controller: false,
+            },
+        );
+        let server = Arc::new(Server::new(control.clone()));
+        runtime.spawn(concord_controller::serve(control.clone()));
+        drop(entered);
+        let hub = Hub::new(runtime.handle().clone(), server);
+        Ok(Core {
+            _runtime: runtime,
+            control,
+            hub,
+        })
+    }
+
+    pub fn control(&self) -> &Arc<Control> {
+        &self.control
+    }
+
+    pub fn hub(&self) -> &Arc<Hub> {
+        &self.hub
+    }
+}
